From 743b6b52ca1089980af108dd9cfa3699b654068f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 13:19:26 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08530/11362] Land exp6199 gemma think-mode A/B: mechanism
 confirmed, mixed result

REQ-ARC-WMTE-6198's actual A/B, after 5 attempts. The first 4 were
corrupted by an infrastructure bug this session tracked down and fixed
across several commits (a Claude Code harness kill of unknown cause, a
CUDA/HIP generator-selection fallback, and finally a 300s-per-attempt
HTTP timeout on LocalGGUFProposer that an earlier env-var fix never
actually reached -- it needed to be passed to the constructor).

The 5th attempt completed cleanly: 10/10 games induced on both arms,
think mode engaged real reasoning on 10/10 calls versus 0/10 for
no_think -- first clean confirmation the mechanism itself works as
designed. adversarial_verify.py: 0 flags.

The result is genuinely mixed, not a clean win either direction:
- heldout_accuracy and goal_predicate_accuracy (signal on ~half the
  roster each) favor think mode consistently; think never loses on
  heldout_accuracy where the arms differ (pooled 0.10 vs 0.37).
- levelup_positive_recall, the metric the auto-generated verdict string
  is keyed on, is thin: 7 of 10 games show 0/0 (no signal), leaving only
  3 informative games, split 1 think / 2 no_think.

Reconciled REQ-ARC-WMTE-6198's spec entry with this honest reading rather
than the bare auto-verdict string. Neither live/scored think-mode default
is flipped by this result alone -- that stays an explicit operator/
A-B-evidence decision per the REQ's own standing convention.

